 # Keys & Trust: Signing, Escrow, Binding, and Separation of Duties
+
+## Plan 019 service identity foundation (2026-10-15)
+
+This proposed request-proof extension does not change repository commit trust,
+key custody, or standard Spaces signatures. No production route enables it.
+The client proof uses an ephemeral credential-bound P-256 did:key. A required
+service assertion uses a separately published service DID verification method
+and binds the exact proof digest, authority, route, and lifetime. No mandatory
+new DID key purpose or historical-key fallback is introduced.
+
+The injected adapter forces trusted DID resolution for every assertion and adds
+no cache. It requires the document ID, exact full method ID, and controller to
+match the expected issuer. Removed methods fail after refreshed resolution.
+Production must verify resolver network bounds and upstream freshness. Any later
+cache needs finite age and capacity, explicit invalidation and rotation visibility;
+stale-on-error acceptance is not permitted. Identity still requires separate
+service authorization. Neither identity nor possession proves human consent.
+
 
